Fonts: three free Google Fonts, each with one job:
Newsreader, a serif, for the lease text, the answers and the memo, the parts you actually read.
Public Sans for the interface: buttons, tabs and labels. It’s the US government’s open-source typeface, plain and trustworthy.
IBM Plex Mono for clause references (§9.3), the Q labels and dates. Monospace makes these look like reference codes rather than prose.
Icons: none in Redline. The logo is just the § character set in Newsreader, and strikethrough text stands in for a “reject” icon. The first version used a few small icons I drew directly as inline SVG.
Layout: plain flexbox and CSS grid. The thin lines between the three panes are a 1px gap showing a grey background through, not borders.
Interactivity: the canvas’s own lightweight template format, used for state and the keep, strike and tab logic. It isn’t a library you’d ship in a real product.

Why it doesn’t read as Tailwind or shadcn
Those defaults bring rounded corners everywhere, soft shadows, slate greys, Inter and an indigo accent. Redline avoids all of them:

Square corners: no border-radius anywhere.
No shadows.
Near-black ink (
#111317) on a cool paper grey (
#E9EAEC).
A single accent color, red (
#C8321B), which only ever means “needs a decision.”

To rebuild it in your own stack, these tokens cover most of the look:

css
:root {
  --ink: #111317;        /* text, settled items, primary buttons */
  --ink-2: #5A5F69;      /* secondary text, struck items */
  --rule: #E3E4E8;       /* hairlines */
  --paper: #FFFFFF;
  --desk: #E9EAEC;       /* behind the document */
  --red: #C8321B;        /* open items only */
  --red-tint: #FBEAE6;   /* open highlight in the document */
  --radius: 0;
  --font-read: 'Newsreader', serif;
  --font-ui: 'Public Sans', sans-serif;
  --font-ref: 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace;
}

You can still use Tailwind with this. Put these values in your Tailwind config in place of the defaults; it’s the defaults that make apps look alike, not Tailwind itself. If you need icons later, Lucide or Phosphor (thin weight) are good fits: use them sparingly and at the ink color, never in the accent red.
